The nonverbal channel carries relationship: how the other sees me, whether I am safe, whether the speaker means what is said. The verbal channel carries content: facts, requests, opinions.
This channel carries the biggest weight for emotional outcomes. How a conversation feels has more to do with the speaker’s posture, eye contact, and gestures than with any single word. The nonverbal signals carry enormous weight for emotion — whether the other feels welcomed, judged, trusted, or dismissed.
Tension is relieved when the speaker has a relaxed, open stance while the listener is leaning back in their chair. Expressions, gestures, posture, and eye contact are all part of one holistic channel. If they are all sending the same message as the words, people relax and believe the speaker.
The numbers come from Albert Mehrabian’s studies in the late 1960’s. Mehrabian never claimed that 93% of all meaning lives outside language, but his numbers have been widely misquoted. The numbers reflect how people judge feelings and attitudes when someone’s words clash with their tone or expression.
Words matter, and the nonverbal channel can’t rescue a dishonest message. But there are still many ways to use body language to keep your real intentions from being lost in translation. Here are a few steps to get started.
